olor:#64AAFA;" fill="#64AAFA" stroke="#64AAFA" stroke-width="1.1381pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 331.82 -36.18 L 331.82 -40.4 L 346.7 -40.4"></path><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#64AAFA;" color="#64AAFA" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-width="1.10371pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 347.08 -40.4)"><path d="M 4.79 0 L 0.76 2.38 L 0 0 L 0.76 -2.38 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#64AAFA;--ltx-fill-color:#64AAFA;" fill="#64AAFA" stroke="#64AAFA" stroke-width="1.1381pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 331.82 -36.18 L 331.82 -64.57 L 318.15 -64.57"></path><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#64AAFA;" color="#64AAFA" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-width="1.10371pt" transform="matrix(-1.0 0.0 0.0 -1.0 317.77 -64.57)"><path d="M 4.79 0 L 0.76 2.38 L 0 0 L 0.76 -2.38 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#64AAFA;--ltx-fill-color:#64AAFA;" fill="#64AAFA" stroke="#64AAFA" stroke-width="1.1381pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 331.82 -36.18 L 331.82 -64.57 L 342.66 -64.57"></path><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#64AAFA;" color="#64AAFA" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-width="1.10371pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 343.04 -64.57)"><path d="M 4.79 0 L 0.76 2.38 L 0 0 L 0.76 -2.38 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#64AAFA;--ltx-fill-color:#64AAFA;" fill="#64AAFA" stroke="#64AAFA" stroke-width="1.1381pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 331.82 14.92 L 331.82 10.15"></path><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#64AAFA;" color="#64AAFA" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-width="1.10371pt" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 331.82 9.77)"><path d="M 4.79 0 L 0.76 2.38 L 0 0 L 0.76 -2.38 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#64AAFA;--ltx-fill-color:#64AAFA;" fill="#64AAFA" stroke="#64AAFA" stroke-width="1.1381pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 69.17 -59.98 L 82.95 -59.98 L 82.95 35.83 L 90.24 35.83"></path><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#64AAFA;" color="#64AAFA" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-width="1.10371pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 90.62 35.83)"><path d="M 4.79 0 L 0.76 2.38 L 0 0 L 0.76 -2.38 Z"></path></g></g></g></svg></span>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Overview of our approach. We construct <span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">BrokenMath</span> by extracting advanced competition theorems, generating paired false versions with an LLM, and verifying them with an expert annotator. State-of-the-art LLMs are then evaluated under an LLM-as-a-judge framework.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">This work: <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">BrokenMath</span> </h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To address this gap, we introduce <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">BrokenMath</span>, the first benchmark for evaluating sycophantic behavior in LLMs in the context of natural language theorem proving. The construction process of <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">BrokenMath</span> is illustrated in <a href="#S1.F1" title="In Limited understanding of sycophancy ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ BrokenMath: A Benchmark for Sycophancy in Theorem Proving with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. First, we collect a diverse set of challenging theorems from advanced mathematics competitions held in 2025, reducing contamination risks. Next, we use an LLM to generate corrupted versions of each theorem that are demonstrably false but plausible. An expert annotator reviews and refines these corrupted statements, discarding cases where no meaningful corruption was found. The resulting dataset contains 504 samples. Importantly, 183 of these are final-answer problems created using our improved methodology, allowing us to compare sycophancy between proof-based and final-answer settings.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:39%;"><img src="2510.04721v1/results_overview.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:209/170;" width="209" height="170" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Results of popular models on <span id="S1.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">BrokenMath</span>. Lower is better.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sycophancy evaluation</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt an LLM-as-a-judge framework to evaluate both state-of-the-art models and agentic systems that use iterative correction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang and Yang, 2025</a>)</cite> or best-of-n techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dekoninck et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Specifically, the judge categorizes each response as one of four categories, ranging from fully sycophantic, where the model attempts to prove the false statement, to ideal, where the model explicitly disproves the false statement and reconstructs the original theorem.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments show that sycophantic behavior is widespread among state-of-the-art LLMs. As shown in <a href="#S1.F2" title="In This work: BrokenMath ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ BrokenMath: A Benchmark for Sycophancy in Theorem Proving with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the best model, <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-5</span>, produces sycophantic answers <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="29\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>29</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">29\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the time. We also find that sycophancy is more pronounced in proof-based problems compared to final-answer ones, and that performance across the two settings is only weakly correlated. Furthermore, results indicate that sycophancy increases significantly with problem difficulty, with models being more likely to accept false premises when they struggle to solve the original problem. These results highlight that <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">BrokenMath</span> is a more challenging and realistic benchmark for sycophancy than prior work, resulting in a more comprehensive understanding of this behavior in LLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px7" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Mitigation strategies</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Various mitigation strategies have been proposed to reduce sycophancy in LLMs, including prompting techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rahman et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and finetuning on non-sycophantic data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xue et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. However, their effectiveness in mathematical reasoning remains unclear due to the limitations of existing benchmarks. We therefore evaluate several strategies on <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">BrokenMath</span>, finding that while they can significantly reduce sycophancy, no method eliminates it.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px8" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Key contributions</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our main contributions are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:2em;">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">BrokenMath</span>, a benchmark for detecting sycophancy in LLM-based proof generation (<a href="#S3" title="3 Benchmark Design and Evaluation ‣ BrokenMath: A Benchmark for Sycophancy in Theorem Proving with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An evaluation of state-of-the-art LLMs, showing sycophantic behavior is widespread (<a href="#S4" title="4 Measuring Sycophantic Behavior ‣ BrokenMath: A Benchmark for Sycophancy in Theorem Proving with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A thorough evaluation of sycophancy mitigation, including prompt design and fine-tuning (<a href="#S5" title="5 Mitigating Sycophantic Behavior ‣ BrokenMath: A Benchmark for Sycophancy in Theorem Proving with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now briefly discuss related work in three areas: theorem proving with LLMs, sycophancy, and uncertainty quantification in mathematical reasoning.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Theorem proving with LLMs</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent benchmarks in mathematical reasoning have increasingly shifted from final-answer problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lightman et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024</a>)</cite> to theorem proving <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Petrov et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dekoninck et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mahdavi et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frieder et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Interestingly, a recurring observation in these works is that LLMs often produce convincing but flawed proofs even for true statements <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Petrov et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dekoninck et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, raising concerns about misplaced trust in model outputs. Our work complements this line of research by investigating sycophantic behavior when models are asked to prove false statements.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sycophancy in LLMs</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Outside of mathematics, sycophancy in LLMs has been widely studied, with research focusing on both its underlying causes and potential mitigations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ranaldi and Pucci, 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Malmqvist, 2024</a>)</cite>. For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al. (2024)</a></cite> show that sycophantic behavior occurs because human feedback and reward models favor persuasive but untruthful responses. In addition, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ranaldi and Pucci (2023a)</a></cite> find that such behavior is especially prevalent when users express strong opinions. Proposed mitigation strategies include finetuning on non-sycophantic data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2023b</a>)</cite>, prompt engineering to explicitly discourage sycophancy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">RRV et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and probing model activations with classifiers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Papadatos and Freedman, 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sycophancy in mathematical reasoning</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A number of benchmarks have been proposed to study sycophancy in mathematical reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xue et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kirichenko et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rahman et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. These benchmarks typically modify existing math word problems by adding or removing constraints, making them contradictory or underspecified. Results consistently show that frontier models are prone to sycophancy. For this reason, several mitigation strategies have been proposed, including reliable prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, fine-tuning on non-sycophantic data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xue et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and probing model activations with classifiers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kirichenko et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. As argued in <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ BrokenMath: A Benchmark for Sycophancy in Theorem Proving with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">BrokenMath</span> improves upon these works by focusing on theorem-proving tasks, using more difficult and less contaminated sources, and using more natural perturbations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Uncertainty quantification in mathematical reasoning</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Uncertainty quantification (UQ) aims to evaluate a model’s confidence in its predictions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. As such, it is closely related to sycophancy as both address the reliability of model outputs. Several works have explored UQ in mathematical reasoning. For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Damani et al., 2025</a>)</cite> fine-tune models to explicitly report uncertainty in their final answers, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mei et al., 2025</a>)</cite> extract confidence estimates from intermediate reasoning traces. More recent works leverage UQ techniques to improve efficiency and accuracy on final-answer problems, such as stopping low-confidence generations early or using uncertainty estimates to select the best candidate among multiple outputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Leang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Our work differs in that we focus on sycophancy, where the model is influenced by an external user, instead of the uncertainty in its internal reasoning.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Benchmark Design and Evaluation</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we describe <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">BrokenMath</span>, a benchmark designed to measure sycophantic behavior in mathematical theorem proving with LLMs. We first describe the benchmark construction process, including dataset curation, sycophantic perturbations, and expert verification (<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Benchmark Construction ‣ 3 Benchmark Design and Evaluation ‣ BrokenMath: A Benchmark for Sycophancy in Theorem Proving with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>). We then outline our evaluation protocol, which relies on an LLM-as-a-judge framework (<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Evaluation protocol ‣ 3 Benchmark Design and Evaluation ‣ BrokenMath: A Benchmark for Sycophancy in Theorem Proving with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Benchmark Construction</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The construction of <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">BrokenMath</span> proceeds in three stages: (1) curating a high-quality dataset of recent mathematical problems, (2) perturbing these problems to form false but plausible theorems, and (3) verifying the quality of these perturbations through expert review.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Problem curation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The foundation of <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">BrokenMath</span> is a corpus of 600+ challenging problems drawn from national and international high-school competitions held in 2025, such as the International Mathematical Olympiad (IMO) and the USA Math Olympiad. Restricting ourselves to recent competitions minimizes the risk of data contamination, since the problems and solutions were released <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">after</span> the knowledge cutoffs of most frontier models. Where official solutions were unavailable, we sourced top-voted community solutions from the Art of Problem Solving (AoPS) forum, each of which was validated by a human expert to ensure correctness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sycophantic perturbations</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We convert each mathematical problem into an invalid theorem that appears plausible. Unlike prior approaches that rely on simply adding or removing constraints in the problem statement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xue et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, our perturbations are more diverse and context-sensitive, tailored to the nature of each problem. Specifically, using the original solution as guidance, we prompted <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-5-mini</span> to generate modified versions that are subtly but demonstrably false. In particular, knowing the original problem and solution should make it easy to see why the perturbed version is incorrect, but without this context, the false statement should appear plausible. By leveraging the solution, we ensure that the perturbation is meaningful and contextually relevant, rather than a random alteration that could be easily dismissed. The prompt, which includes detailed instructions and few-shot examples to guide the process, is provided in <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1 Sycophantic Rephrasing ‣ Appendix D Prompts ‣ BrokenMath: A Benchmark for Sycophancy in Theorem Proving with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S3.F3.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:397.5pt;height:214.5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-2.1pt,1.1pt) scale(0.989471096490082,0.989471096490082) ;"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.F3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="299.89" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 546.63 299.89" width="546.63"><g transform="translate(0,299.89) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(87.17,0) translate(0,149.95)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#D8D8F0;--ltx-fill-color:#F5F5FF;" fill="#F5F5FF" stroke="#D8D8F0" stroke-width="0.8pt"><path d="M 81.08 142.13 L -81.08 142.13 C -84.14 142.13 -86.61 139.65 -86.61 136.59 L -86.61 -136.59 C -86.61 -139.65 -84.14 -142.13 -81.08 -142.13 L 81.08 -142.13 C 84.14 -142.13 86.61 -139.65 86.61 -136.59 L 86.61 136.59 C 86.61 139.65 84.14 142.13 81.08 142.13 Z M -86.61 -142.13"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.8pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -74.8 120.82)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:10.81em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:18.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="260.64" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.49)" width="149.58"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
